<h2>Itaewon — Seoul's Global Neighborhood</h2>
<p>Itaewon is unlike anywhere else in Seoul. Built up around the US military base (Yongsan Garrison), it developed into the city's most cosmopolitan district — a place where you can eat Turkish kebabs, Mexican tacos, Lebanese mezze, and Italian pasta within a five-minute walk. It's where Seoul's expat community congregates, where LGBTQ+ venues exist openly, and where the nightlife runs longer and louder than almost anywhere else in the city.</p>

<h2>🍽️ Food in Itaewon</h2>

<h3>International Cuisine</h3>
<p>Itaewon's main street and surrounding alleys offer genuine international food diversity rare in Korea:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Vatos Urban Tacos:</strong> Korean-Mexican fusion that actually works. Kimchi carnitas, galbi tacos. Very popular — expect a wait.</li>
  <li><strong>Maple Tree House:</strong> Premium Korean BBQ in a stunning converted hanok building. Wagyu and premium cuts.</li>
  <li><strong>Bab (밥):</strong> Award-winning Korean fine dining.</li>
  <li><strong>La Plancha:</strong> Excellent Spanish tapas bar. Good wine list.</li>
  <li><strong>Haon Guest House:</strong> Traditional Korean food with great views.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Halal Food</h3>
<p>Itaewon has Korea's largest concentration of halal restaurants, centered around the Seoul Central Mosque (이태원 이슬람 성원). The mosque area (often called "Halal Street") has Turkish, Middle Eastern, Pakistani, and Malaysian restaurants with certified halal menus.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Food tip:</strong> Itaewon's prices are slightly higher than other Seoul neighborhoods — this reflects the premium ingredients and international character. Budget ₩15,000–₩40,000 for a solid meal at most restaurants.
</div>

<h2>🍸 Nightlife</h2>
<p>Itaewon's nightlife is the most international in Seoul, with a range from laid-back craft beer bars to full club nights.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Craftworks Taproom:</strong> Seoul's best craft beer bar. Korean-brewed craft beers, great burgers, lively atmosphere.</li>
  <li><strong>Why Not Bar:</strong> LGBTQ+ friendly bar, welcoming to everyone.</li>
  <li><strong>Cakeshop:</strong> Top electronic music club. International DJs, serious sound system.</li>
  <li><strong>Soap:</strong> Underground club with a great music policy.</li>
  <li><strong>Fountain:</strong> Rooftop bar with spectacular views of Seoul.</li>
</ul>

<h2>🛍️ Shopping in Itaewon</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Antique Furniture Street:</strong> Near Itaewon Station, dealers selling Korean and Asian antiques and furniture</li>
  <li><strong>Custom tailoring:</strong> Several tailors offer fast, affordable custom suits and shirts — a legacy of the military-era tourism</li>
  <li><strong>Itaewon main street boutiques:</strong> Independent fashion stores, especially near the Hamilton Hotel</li>
  <li><strong>Foreign food grocery stores:</strong> Several shops carry imported ingredients hard to find elsewhere in Seoul</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Safety note:</strong> Itaewon is generally very safe. Exercise normal urban caution late at night, especially during busy weekends. Keep your belongings secure in crowded clubs.
</div>

<h2>📍 Nearby: Haebangchon (HBC) & Gyeongnidan</h2>
<p>Two adjacent neighborhoods worth exploring:</p>
<p><strong>Haebangchon (해방촌):</strong> The hill above Itaewon. Quieter, more residential, great independent cafes and bars. Feels like a village within the city.</p>
<p><strong>Gyeongnidan-gil (경리단길):</strong> A trendy street of independent restaurants, wine bars, and boutiques. Excellent for dinner and drinks in a less touristy setting.</p>

